From 2dd6b5e869e39ed42e22b2c4ebca4cb22ee26579 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:16:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Resolution: a whole-number multiple of the frame rate keeps
 the display mode (whitelist)

With a whitelist, Kodi takes a mode at the exact frame rate of a video, then one at twice it or
at 2.5 times it where its options allow them. A display at 120 Hz then changes its mode for 24 fps
video, to 24 Hz or, with 3:2 pulldown, to 60 Hz, where 120 Hz already shows each picture the same
number of times (five). CoreELEC has an option for this (videoscreen.whitelistwholenumber: 4 and
5 times the frame rate, with its own order of the searches).

This adds videoscreen.hadeswholemultiple (System > Display, under the whitelist, off by default).
With it on and a whitelist, when the whitelist has no mode at the exact frame rate, neither at the
size of the video nor at the width of the GUI, the mode the display is in is kept where:
- it is in the whitelist, progressive and not 3D, of the size of the video or of the width of
  the GUI;
- its refresh rate is k times the frame rate (within Kodi's 0.01 Hz), k >= 3, or k = 2 where
  Kodi's option of double refresh rates allows them: 120 Hz for 24 fps, 119.88 Hz for 23.976 fps,
  100 Hz for 25 fps; not 120 Hz for 23.976 fps;
- the video is known to be progressive (its frame rate is then the exact one) and is SDR or
  HDR10: never Dolby Vision (in the stream, in a picture, or as native or measured output, which
  keeps its cadence, at 60 Hz at most), HDR10+ or HLG.
Otherwise the search goes on as Kodi's, step for step; with the setting off, or without a
whitelist, nothing changes. The rule is a function of WholeMultiple.h, which a host test runs.

What the video is, VideoPlayer tells for each video stream that opens, and its pictures only make
it worse within a file (Dolby Vision, HDR10+ or HLG found in one): HDR10+ metadata that the
demuxer did not see in the first packets make the mode be chosen again at the first picture that
carries them.

kodi.log says when a mode is kept ("[WHITELIST] Hades: ... kept for ...") and why a mode that is
a whole-number multiple is not ("... is not kept as a whole-number multiple: ...").

HDR10 keeps a mode only where the link is known, with certainty, to carry 10 bits per component
or more in it with HDR10 on; where that is not known, the mode changes as Kodi chooses it. What is
known comes from the driver, and is kept for the mode (its timing), the display (a hash of its
EDID) and the settings that change the driver's choice (HDR where RGB would be 8-bit, HDR output
depth), for the session (WholeMultipleDepthMemory):
- i915 and amdgpu with the Hades patches: while the GUI is shown alone, with the setting on and a
  whitelist, once per mode and display, a TEST_ONLY commit of HDR10 in the mode in use
  (HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA with the PQ EOTF, Colorspace BT2020_RGB, the "max bpc" Kodi sets for HDR
  where it sets one, the choice of HDR in YCbCr 4:2:0, and the HDR10 depth guard,
  DVBRIDGE_HDR10_DEEP_COLOR). The driver answers with the format and the depth of that
  configuration (DVBRIDGE_LINK_FORMAT), or refuses it below 10 bits. Both drivers treat a change
  of the HDR metadata as a modeset, so the stream that is checked is the one HDR10 would get;
- every driver, once HDR10 is on the output (ProbeOutputDepth()): NV_OUTPUT_BPC on nvidia-drm,
  the guard on i915 and amdgpu. NVKMS chooses the depth and HDMI FRL only when it commits, and
  says nothing of a TEST_ONLY commit: on NVIDIA, HDR10 keeps a mode only after HDR10 went out at
  10 bits or more in it.
Where a mode kept for HDR10 is then measured below 10 bits, the mode is chosen again.

kodi.log says what was known: "... kept ..., and the link carries 10 bits or more in it with
HDR10 (N bits, <how it is known>)", "... is not kept as a whole-number multiple: the video is
HDR10, and whether the link carries 10 bits in this mode is not known (...)", and the answer of
the TEST_ONLY commit ("HDR output: HDR10 in <mode> would go out as <format> at N bits ...").
